WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:14.003
FIRST LIGHT · SOLUTION
Bridging a broken trace
Diagnosis (see "How to approach"): R1 → D1 reads OL, and half-splitting puts the break on the right-hand trace.
Fix: bridge the gap with solder, check the LED, then verify on the test bench.
Tools: Soldering iron (3) · Select (1) · Run tests

2
00:00:15.364 --> 00:00:26.816
STEP 1 · LOCATE
Here it is: a hairline gap in the copper.
Why: Flexing, a scratch or corrosion can crack a trace. The gap is tiny, but no current crosses it.

3
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:41.121
STEP 2 · REPAIR
Soldering iron (3): click the gap to flow a solder bridge across it.
Why: Solder conducts. A blob that wets the copper on both sides restores the path. For wider gaps on a real board you would add a short piece of wire.

4
00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:54.311
STEP 3 · CHECK
Press SW1: D1 lights, and the supply now shows about 10 mA.
Why: Ohm's law: the cell gives 3 V, the red LED drops about 1.9 V, so about 1.1 V is left across R1 (100 Ω): 1.1 V / 100 Ω ≈ 11 mA.

5
00:00:54.312 --> 00:01:09.738
STEP 4 · VERIFY
Run the test bench (Run tests).
Why: It powers the board, checks every objective, and only decrypts the flag if the circuit now matches the original design: a fix that merely works around a fault does not count.

6
00:01:09.739 --> 00:01:13.342
STEP 4 · VERIFY
All objectives pass and the flag is captured. Earn your own: it is blurred here.

7
00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:27.344
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Repair and prove it
A broken trace is bridged with solder (small gaps) or a jumper wire (long ones).
Check the repair with a number you can predict: here I = V / R gives the LED current.
Finish on the test bench: it proves the circuit, not just the symptom.
